'''Teixits Tècnics de Balboa, S.A.''' (TTB) is a Balboan {{w|technical textile|performance fabric}} contract manufacturer based in [[Sant Bart]]. The company specialises in high-performance fabrics with functional properties — including {{w|moisture-wicking|moisture management}}, {{w|breathable fabric|breathability}}, {{w|stretch fabric|stretch}}, {{w|ultraviolet|UV}} protection, {{w|flame retardant|flame resistance}}, and {{w|antimicrobial}} treatments — produced on behalf of international sportswear, outdoor, and industrial brands serving the [[BTPMEU]] market. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker TTB and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index.

TTB is 25 per cent owned by [[Grup Ella]], a Volisanian clothing manufacturer. The investment gives Grup Ella guaranteed access to TTB's performance fabric production capacity for its athleisure sub-brand Gir and its sports brand Massiu. Through this relationship, TTB-manufactured fabrics and finished products are exported worldwide, as Gir and Massiu distribute across Volisanian and international retail networks beyond the BTPMEU market.

== History ==

TTB was founded in 1996 by Laura Bosch i Areny, a textile engineer who had previously led the technical textiles division at [[Tèxtils Nacionals Balboa]]. Bosch identified that the growing demand for performance fabrics from international sportswear and outdoor brands was not being met by existing Balboan manufacturers, which remained focused on commodity textile production. She left TNB to establish a dedicated performance fabric company, initially operating from a leased facility in [[Sant Bart]].

The company's early years coincided with the establishment of the [[BTPMEU]] in 1998, which created a tariff advantage for brands manufacturing within the economic union. TTB positioned itself as a specialised contract manufacturer, offering performance fabric production capabilities that international brands could use to supply the BTPMEU market without maintaining their own manufacturing facilities in the region.

Initial contracts focused on {{w|moisture-wicking|moisture-managing}} fabrics for sportswear brands, using synthetic fibers sourced from [[Opal (Company)|Opal]] and [[Lycan (Company)|Lycan]]. In 2001, TTB developed and patented a proprietary {{w|moisture-wicking|moisture-transporting}} weave structure — a multi-layered cross-section that actively draws perspiration away from the skin through capillary action more efficiently than standard moisture-wicking fabrics. The patent, registered internationally, is licensed exclusively to Grup Ella's Gir and Massiu brands and is available to other clients on a selective basis. The proprietary weave became a key differentiator for the company and a primary reason for Grup Ella's 25 per cent acquisition in 2004.

The company expanded into {{w|breathable fabric|breathable membrane}} laminates, {{w|stretch fabric|stretch fabrics}}, and {{w|ultraviolet|UV-protective}} textiles in the early 2000s, broadening its client base to include outdoor and activewear brands.

TTB was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 2005. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] acquired a 7 per cent stake in 2010 as part of its investment in higher-value manufacturing.

In the 2010s, TTB expanded into industrial performance fabrics, producing {{w|flame retardant|flame-resistant}} textiles for protective clothing and {{w|antimicrobial}} fabrics for medical and hygiene applications. The company also invested in {{w|textile manufacturing|digital textile printing}} and laser-cutting capabilities to serve clients requiring short production runs and rapid prototyping.

== Patents ==

TTB holds an international patent on a proprietary {{w|moisture-wicking|moisture-transporting}} weave structure, developed in 2001 by founder Laura Bosch i Areny and her engineering team. The weave uses a multi-layered cross-section with differential fiber densities — a tight inner layer against the skin, a porous transport channel, and a dispersed outer layer — that creates a sustained capillary gradient, drawing moisture away from the body more rapidly and consistently than standard {{w|moisture-wicking}} treatments applied to conventional fabrics. Unlike surface treatments that degrade with washing, the moisture-transporting property is structural, inherent to the weave itself.

The patent is licensed exclusively to [[Grup Ella]]'s Gir and Massiu brands, and is available to other contract manufacturing clients on a selective basis under licence terms negotiated with TTB. The proprietary weave is a primary differentiator in TTB's contract manufacturing portfolio and a significant factor in the company's higher margins relative to commodity fabric producers.

== Operations ==

TTB operates a single production facility in [[Sant Bart]], comprising:

* Warp-knitting and weft-knitting lines for stretch and structured fabrics
* Laminating lines for {{w|breathable fabric|breathable membrane}} composites
* Chemical finishing lines for {{w|moisture-wicking|moisture management}}, {{w|ultraviolet|UV protection}}, {{w|flame retardant|flame resistance}}, and {{w|antimicrobial}} treatments
* {{w|Textile printing|Digital printing}} and laser-cutting for short-run production

The company sources synthetic fiber from [[Opal (Company)|Opal]] and [[Lycan (Company)|Lycan]], and {{w|membrane (textile)|membrane}} materials from specialised suppliers. Approximately 50 per cent of output is exported to BTPMEU-based brands, with 30 per cent exported worldwide through Grup Ella's Gir and Massiu distribution networks, and the remaining 20 per cent supplying domestic manufacturers.

== Position in the textile sector ==

TTB occupies a distinct niche within the Balboan textile sector, separate from the commodity fabric production of [[Tèxtils Nacionals Balboa]] and the integrated mill operations of [[Horaci Eduard Molí]]. While TNB and HEM produce a broad range of woven and knitted fabrics, TTB focuses exclusively on performance fabrics with specific functional properties, commanding higher margins per metre but serving a narrower client base. The company does not compete directly with [[Acabats i Tints de Balboa]] in wet processing, as TTB's finishing operations are specialised for performance treatments rather than commodity dyeing and printing.

